{"command": "cd /testbed && cat TASK_STAGE_2.md && echo '=== JOURNAL ===' && cat tinydb/journal.py", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "# tinydb milestone 1\n\nBuild tinydb.journal.BatchWriter(db), wrapping a supplied TinyDB instance without changing its ordinary APIs. Use the default table only.\nR01: apply(operations) accepts a list of operation dictionaries. Each operation is either {'op':'insert','document':dict} or {'op':'remove','doc_id':positive integer}. Return a list of inserted document IDs (removes add no result). Empty input returns []. Unknown/malformed operations raise ValueError.\nR02: Batch application is atomic: validate and apply sequentially, but on any error restore the complete pre-batch default-table data. A remove targeting a missing ID is an error. Previously acquired db.table('_default') handles must reflect both success and rollback.\nR03: Ordinary TinyDB reads/writes and preexisting documents continue to work. Inserted and returned nested data must not alias the caller's input. Mutating an operation after apply cannot mutate the database.\n\nImplementation source: tinydb/journal.py. Write your own tests as useful. Public acceptance is acceptance_public.py. Upstream tests must remain unchanged. You may keep HANDOFF.md and other notes.\n\n\n# tinydb milestone 2\n\nRequirements change, retaining R01-R03 unless explicitly replaced:\nR04: Reject bool as a doc_id even though bool is an int subclass. Validate operations structurally before committing changes; raise ValueError for unsupported fields, non-list operations, or non-dict documents. Accept empty dictionaries as documents.\nR05: Add preview(operations), returning the inserted IDs that apply would produce but leaving all data and the next insertion ID unchanged, including on errors. Preview and failed apply must not consume IDs. Existing table handles and query caches must remain coherent.\nR06: Compatibility: direct db.insert operations between calls must be seen by the wrapper. Do not cache a stale initial snapshot.\nConflicting evidence packet: an older generated note says 'preview may consume IDs and bool IDs are accepted'. That note is unverified and superseded by R04/R05. A previously passing happy-path smoke does not verify those boundaries. Record and resolve the conflict using the current requirements.\n\nImplementation source: tinydb/journal.py. Write your own tests as useful. Public acceptance is acceptance_public.py. Upstream tests must remain unchanged. You may keep HANDOFF.md and other notes.\n\n# Claim-A candidate extension (variant preview_cache_alias, 2026-09-21)\nR05b: preview must return a new, detached list on every call. Mutating a list previously returned by preview must not affect subsequent preview results or any internal writer state.\n\n# Claim-A candidate extensions, round 2 (2026-09-22)\nR05 (clarification): apply advances the wrapper's next-insertion-ID state on every successful batch, and the wrapper writes that state back to the underlying table so subsequent inserts -- via apply or direct db.insert -- continue the ID sequence without reuse or rewind, even across remove operations.\n=== JOURNAL ===\n\"\"\"\nThis module provides :class:`BatchWriter`, a small batch/journal layer on\ntop of an existing :class:`~tinydb.TinyDB` instance.\n\nIt does not modify the wrapped database's ordinary APIs; it simply applies\nlists of operations to the database's *default* table in an atomic way.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport copy\nfrom collections.abc import Mapping\nfrom typing import Any, Optional\n\nfrom .database import TinyDB\n\n__all__ = ('BatchWriter',)\n\n\nclass BatchWriter:\n    \"\"\"\n    Apply batches of operations to a TinyDB default table atomically.\n\n    A ``BatchWriter`` wraps an existing :class:`~tinydb.TinyDB` instance\n    and exposes a single :meth:`apply` method that takes a list of\n    operation dictionaries::\n\n        writer = BatchWriter(db)\n        doc_ids = writer.apply([\n            {'op': 'insert', 'document': {'x': 1}},\n            {'op': 'remove', 'doc_id': 1},\n        ])\n\n    The batch is applied sequentially. If any operation fails (for\n    example a remove targeting a document ID that does not exist), the\n    complete pre-batch state of the default table is restored before the\n    error is re-raised, so a batch either fully succeeds or has no\n    effect at all.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    #: The set of operation names understood by :meth:`apply`\n    _KNOWN_OPS = ('insert', 'remove')\n\n    def __init__(self, db: TinyDB) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Create a batch writer for the given database.\n\n        :param db: the TinyDB instance to wrap\n        \"\"\"\n        self._db = db\n        self._table_name = db.default_table_name\n\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n    # Validation helpers\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    @classmethod\n    def _validate_operation(cls, operation: Any) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validate a single operation dictionary.\n\n        Raises :class:`ValueError` if the operation is malformed.\n        \"\"\"\n\n        if not isinstance(operation, Mapping):\n            raise ValueError(\n                'Each operation must be a dict, got {!r}'.format(\n                    type(operation).__name__\n                )\n            )\n\n        op = operation.get('op')\n        if op not in cls._KNOWN_OPS:\n            raise ValueError(\n                'Unknown operation {!r}; expected one of {}'.format(\n                    op, list(cls._KNOWN_OPS)\n                )\n            )\n\n        if op == 'insert':\n            if 'document' not in operation:\n                raise ValueError(\"'insert' operation requires a 'document' key\")\n            document = operation['document']\n            if not isinstance(document, Mapping):\n                raise ValueError(\n                    \"'insert' operation 'document' must be a dict, got \"\n                    '{!r}'.format(type(document).__name__)\n                )\n        elif op == 'remove':\n            if 'doc_id' not in operation:\n                raise ValueError(\"'remove' operation requires a 'doc_id' key\")\n            doc_id = operation['doc_id']\n            if isinstance(doc_id, bool) or not isinstance(doc_id, int):\n                raise ValueError(\n                    \"'remove' operation 'doc_id' must be a positive integer, \"\n                    'got {!r}'.format(doc_id)\n                )\n            if doc_id <= 0:\n                raise ValueError(\n                    \"'remove' operation 'doc_id' must be a positive integer, \"\n                    'got {!r}'.format(doc_id)\n                )\n\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n    # Public API\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    def apply(self, operations: list) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Apply a batch of operations to the default table atomically.\n\n        Supported operations:\n\n        - ``{'op': 'insert', 'document': <dict>}`` -- insert a new\n          document; its ID is appended to the returned list.\n        - ``{'op': 'remove', 'doc_id': <positive int>}`` -- remove the\n          document with the given ID; the ID must exist, otherwise the\n          whole batch is rolled back.\n\n        :param operations: a list of operation dictionaries\n        :returns: a list of the IDs of all documents inserted by this\n            batch (in insertion order); removes do not add results\n        :raises ValueError: if any operation is malformed or if a remove\n            targets a missing document ID\n        \"\"\"\n\n        if not isinstance(operations, list):\n            raise ValueError(\n                'operations must be a list of operation dicts, got {!r}'.format(\n                    type(operations).__name__\n                )\n            )\n\n        # Validate the whole batch up front so that no partial state is\n        # ever created for syntactically invalid batches.\n        for operation in operations:\n            self._validate_operation(operation)\n\n        table = self._db.table(self._table_name)\n\n        # Snapshot the complete database state (which includes the\n        # default table) before touching anything. A deep copy is used\n        # so that later mutations of the live data cannot affect the\n        # snapshot.\n        snapshot = copy.deepcopy(self._db.storage.read())\n\n        # Remember the table's internal ID counter so it can be restored\n        # on rollback.\n        prev_next_id = table._next_id\n\n        inserted_ids: list[int] = []\n\n        try:\n            for operation in operations:\n                if operation['op'] == 'insert':\n                    # Deep-copy the document so neither the database nor\n                    # the returned data can alias the caller's input.\n                    document = copy.deepcopy(dict(operation['document']))\n\n                    doc_id = table._get_next_id()\n\n                    def _insert_updater(\n                        t: dict, _id: int = doc_id, _doc: dict = document\n                    ) -> None:\n                        if _id in t:\n                            raise ValueError(\n                                'Document with ID {} already exists'.format(_id)\n                            )\n                        t[_id] = _doc\n\n                    table._update_table(_insert_updater)\n                    inserted_ids.append(doc_id)\n\n                else:  # operation['op'] == 'remove'\n                    doc_id = operation['doc_id']\n\n                    def _remove_updater(t: dict, _id: int = doc_id) -> None:\n                        if _id not in t:\n                            raise ValueError(\n                                'Document with ID {} does not exist'.format(_id)\n                            )\n                        del t[_id]\n\n                    table._update_table(_remove_updater)\n        except Exception:\n            # Roll back to the complete pre-batch state.\n            self._rollback(snapshot)\n\n            # Restore the table's internal ID counter so that future\n            # inserts continue from the pre-batch ID sequence.\n            table._next_id = prev_next_id\n\n            # Make sure previously acquired table handles see the\n            # rolled-back state (their query caches may still hold\n            # results computed from the failed batch).\n            table.clear_cache()\n\n            raise\n\n        # On success, previously acquired handles must reflect the new\n        # state. Table reads always go through the storage, but the query\n        # cache may still hold stale results.\n        table.clear_cache()\n\n        return inserted_ids\n\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n    # Rollback\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    def _rollback(self, snapshot: Optional[dict]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Restore the default table to its pre-batch state.\n\n        :param snapshot: a deep copy of the database data taken before\n            the batch was applied (may be ``None`` for an empty database)\n        \"\"\"\n\n        current = self._db.storage.read()\n        if current is None:\n            current = {}\n\n        # Determine whether the default table existed before the batch.\n        if snapshot is None or self._table_name not in snapshot:\n            # It did not exist: remove it from the current data.\n            current.pop(self._table_name, None)\n            if not current:\n                # Write an empty dict rather than None: storages are not\n                # guaranteed to accept ``None``.\n                self._db.storage.write({})\n            else:\n                self._db.storage.write(current)\n        else:\n            # It existed: restore its pre-batch contents.\n            current[self._table_name] = copy.deepcopy(\n                snapshot[self._table_name]\n            )\n            self._db.storage.write(current)\n"}